  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>A career in Navy after 12th science runs through two real after-12th doors — NDA (UPSC written
    exam plus SSB) and the 10+2 (B.Tech) Cadet Entry Scheme (JEE Main score plus SSB) — both leading to a
    commissioned officer career with a fully funded technical or general-service training pipeline, not
    one single "join the Navy" exam.</strong> The honest decision filter is not just which exam to clear.
    It is whether you can genuinely sustain a multi-stage selection process, years of hierarchy and sea
    postings, and a fixed government pay ceiling in exchange for stability, respect, and — used well — a
    genuine <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> that opens stronger second-career options later.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>NDA (UPSC exam) and the 10+2 (B.Tech) Cadet Entry Scheme (JEE Main-based, no separate Navy written exam) are the two after-12th officer-entry doors into the Indian Navy.</li>
      <li>This is the armed-forces Indian Navy, not Merchant Navy — a genuinely different career with different exams, employers, and pay structure. See the disambiguation below if you meant commercial shipping.</li>
      <li><strong>Selection is a genuine multi-stage filter: a written exam or JEE cutoff, then the SSB interview over several days, then a medical exam — clearing one stage does not mean the job is nearly done.</strong></li>
      <li>Officer pay runs on the 7th CPC defence matrix from Level 10 upward, plus Military Service Pay of Rs 15,500 a month — respectable and secure, but fixed and rank-linked, not open-ended.</li>
      <li>Executive branch carries the widest in-service ceiling (only this branch reaches ship command and Chief of the Naval Staff); Engineering branch carries a genuine post-retirement ceiling through lateral entry into Merchant Navy senior engineering ranks.</li>
      <li>Build a second, high-income skill portfolio alongside service — it is what turns a fixed-tenure or post-retirement exit into earlier financial freedom instead of a scramble.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

